You can leverage this *a lot* — but only if you package it as **“investment judgment + finance execution”**, not “cool side project”.

From your README, you already have the right building blocks: a **proper DCF model**, **WACC (CAPM + after-tax debt)**, historical “hints”, user assumptions, and a clean **FastAPI + React** productized setup using Financial Modeling Prep data. ([GitHub][1])

Here’s how to turn it into career leverage (what hiring managers / investors actually respond to):

---

## 1) Turn the tool into **credible investing evidence**

### Add 3–5 public “case studies” (this is the big unlock)

For each company:

* **Thesis** (why it’s mispriced)
* Key assumptions (growth, margin, reinvestment, risk premium)
* Output (intrinsic value vs price)
* **Variant views** (base/bull/bear) + what would change your mind
* Post-mortem updates monthly (did reality track assumptions?)

Put these in `/docs` or a simple public Notion/Medium/Substack and link from the repo.

Why this matters: finance hiring is “show me” — **models + memos** beat certificates.

---

## 2) Make it look like an **investment workflow**, not a calculator

Your current endpoints (GET company data, POST valuation) are great. ([GitHub][1])
Now add workflow features that mirror real analyst work:

**Must-have additions**

* **Sensitivity tables** (WACC vs terminal growth, margin vs growth)
* **Scenario manager** (save base/bull/bear, compare)
* **Assumption audit trail** (what changed, when, why)
* **Quality checks** (WACC > g guardrails, negative FCF handling, warning banners)
* **Data provenance** (show exactly which fields came from the API vs user inputs)

These scream “I understand investment risk and process”.

---

## 3) Make it a **signal of judgment under uncertainty** (AI-proof angle)

AI can generate a DCF. What’s scarce is:

* choosing assumptions that reflect the business reality
* knowing which drivers matter
* communicating risks clearly

So: ship a “**What drives value**” panel:

* value attribution by driver (margin, reinvestment, growth duration, discount rate)
* “most sensitive inputs” ranking
* narrative prompts (“If your thesis is X, your model should show Y…”)

That’s you demonstrating investor thinking.

---

## 4) Productize it so people can *touch* it

A repo alone is passive. A demo is leverage.

Do one:

* Hosted demo (even limited) + screenshots/gif
* A short Loom video (3–5 min): “Here’s how I value a company in 5 minutes”
* Add a simple “Try AAPL / MSFT” quick-start button

This turns it into a **portfolio piece** you can send in outreach.

---

## 5) Use it to open doors with **the right target roles**

This project is most valuable for:

* **Equity research / public markets analyst**
* **Tech-focused VC / growth equity associate**
* **Corporate development / strategy & investments**
* **FinTech / systematic fundamentals tooling teams**

When you message people, lead with:

> “I built a DCF + WACC valuation tool (FastAPI/React) and wrote investment memos using it. I’d love 15 minutes to understand what your team looks for in entry candidates.”

Attach 1 memo + the demo link. That’s a *serious* approach.

---

## 6) Put it on your CV the *finance* way (not engineering way)

Bad bullet:

* “Built full-stack stock valuation tool in FastAPI/React”

Good bullets:

* “Built DCF valuation engine with revenue-driven FCF projections and WACC (CAPM + after-tax debt) to estimate intrinsic value per share.” ([GitHub][1])
* “Implemented scenario/sensitivity analysis and assumption controls to quantify valuation risk and key drivers.”
* “Authored investment memos based on model outputs (base/bull/bear), tracking forecast vs realised results.”

---

## A concrete “next 30 days” leverage plan

1. Publish **2 investment memos** (one compounder, one cyclical)
2. Add **sensitivity + scenario saving**
3. Record **one demo video**
4. Do **20 targeted outreaches** (buy-side/VC/corp dev) with memo + demo

---

